1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:08,145
Give an AI agent a company card, and it can overspend, get talked into a bigger order by a web page, or charge you twice.

2
00:00:08,445 --> 00:00:10,070
Better prompts don't fix that.

3
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:17,868
So I built agentpay, a purchasing agent on Stripe Issuing, where the model proposes and code decides.

4
00:00:18,168 --> 00:00:26,435
Across 88 test scenarios, including 18 attacks, it made zero unsafe purchases and zero duplicate charges.

5
00:00:27,685 --> 00:00:33,118
A teammate asks for three USB-C docks, and Claude plans the order with read-only tools.

6
00:00:33,818 --> 00:00:39,762
Code checks policy and budget, then issues a single-use card for exactly a hundred and eighty dollars.

7
00:00:40,462 --> 00:00:44,085
Once it's charged, the card is cancelled, so a second charge fails.

8
00:00:45,335 --> 00:00:49,793
Now an attack. The product page tells the model to order five, and it falls for it.

9
00:00:50,493 --> 00:00:57,691
It doesn't matter. Code sees the five came from a web page, not the user, so a human reviews it, and no card is created.

10
00:00:58,941 --> 00:01:04,607
On the merchant side, a twenty dollar charge on a fifteen dollar card is declined by Stripe itself.

11
00:01:05,307 --> 00:01:09,579
A look-alike merchant is declined by my real-time authorization check.

12
00:01:10,829 --> 00:01:14,869
The key decision: the model never holds a card or approves anything.

13
00:01:15,569 --> 00:01:21,653
Code checks where each value came from, then policy, risk and budget, and the card is locked to the exact amount.

14
00:01:22,903 --> 00:01:28,476
An eval suite fails CI on any unsafe purchase, alongside 167 tests.

15
00:01:29,176 --> 00:01:34,934
A code review still found 8 bugs the evals missed, so I fixed each one with a regression test.

16
00:01:36,184 --> 00:01:45,844
I built a purchasing agent with zero unsafe purchases across 88 scenarios, by letting the model only propose, while tested code and single-use cards decide.

